WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.985
SOC 2 reports are organized around Trust Services Criteria.

2
00:00:03.985 --> 00:00:11.956
The Security category is commonly included because nearly every SOC 2
report needs to address protection against unauthorized

3
00:00:11.956 --> 00:00:16.828
access, unauthorized disclosure, and damage to systems that support the
service.

4
00:00:16.828 --> 00:00:23.470
Other categories may be included depending on the service and the
commitments made to customers.

5
00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.456
Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, and Privacy are not
automatic.

6
00:00:27.456 --> 00:00:34.541
They should be included when they match the service, contractual promises,
risk profile, and customer expectations.

7
00:00:34.541 --> 00:00:37.641
Scope is where SOC 2 becomes concrete.

8
00:00:37.641 --> 00:00:43.398
The scope defines which service or system is included, which environments
are relevant,

9
00:00:43.398 --> 00:00:48.712
which people and teams operate the controls, which processes support the
service,

10
00:00:48.712 --> 00:00:52.254
and which controls map to the selected criteria.

11
00:00:52.254 --> 00:01:00.225
A cloud platform, a customer-facing SaaS application, an internal support
process, and a data processing workflow can all

12
00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:02.025
have different scope boundaries.

13
00:01:02.025 --> 00:01:07.782
Good scope connects the service being sold to the promises made to
customers.

14
00:01:07.782 --> 00:01:11.767
If customers rely on uptime commitments, availability may matter.

15
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:16.196
If the service stores confidential customer information, confidentiality
may matter.

16
00:01:16.196 --> 00:01:22.838
If the service processes transactions where completeness and accuracy are
central, processing integrity may matter.

17
00:01:22.838 --> 00:01:28.152
If the service collects personal information under privacy commitments,
privacy may matter.

18
00:01:28.152 --> 00:01:34.795
The scope should explain these relationships without pretending the report
covers more than it does.

19
00:01:34.795 --> 00:01:36.595
Weak scope creates confusion.

20
00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:42.352
If the report includes a control but not the system customers care about,

21
00:01:42.352 --> 00:01:46.337
the report may not answer the real risk question.

22
00:01:46.337 --> 00:01:52.537
If the report describes a service broadly but evidence covers only a small
environment,

23
00:01:52.537 --> 00:01:56.522
the team may struggle during audit and customer review.

24
00:01:56.522 --> 00:02:03.165
If commitments are vague, teams may collect evidence that does not connect
to the criteria.

25
00:02:03.165 --> 00:02:08.479
Scope is not just paperwork; it drives evidence, ownership, and report
usefulness.

26
00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:14.235
Technical and operational teams should understand scope because their work
may become evidence.

27
00:02:14.235 --> 00:02:22.206
Access to production, change management for customer-facing systems,
incident response for the service, vendor dependencies, backup processes,
monitoring,

28
00:02:22.206 --> 00:02:26.192
and onboarding or offboarding may all be in scope.

29
00:02:26.192 --> 00:02:32.834
Teams need to know which systems and processes matter so they can operate
controls consistently.

30
00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:39.034
The practical lesson is to define scope early and keep it aligned with
reality.

31
00:02:39.034 --> 00:02:45.234
Document the service, boundaries, dependencies, selected Trust Services
Categories, customer commitments, and control owners.

32
00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:53.147
When the scope is clear, Type I readiness and Type II evidence collection
become much easier to manage.
